from common import *
it('KERNEL-0254','rule','3.6','Chain classes - mandatory: coast, shore, bank, river, wall trace, arterial, band and kerb edges, plot lines, user-drawn geometry cannot fail for a precision reason: after attraction and placement no precision error is left, only §3.1 non-precision codes can reject one, and a corner below minFaceAngle commits with W_ACUTE_CORNER.',['§3.6 Chain classes','§3.2 (2) minFaceAngle hard or soft'],'kernel',['@insertChain cls'],['W_ACUTE_CORNER','§4 stages inserting mandatory chains','§7.1 user drawing'],['mandatory chain','chain class'])
it('KERNEL-0255','rule','3.6','Chain classes - optional: growth proposals, optional lanes, decorative lines are also rejected with E_BELOW_MIN_ANGLE; with E_BELOW_MIN_EDGE when the chain collapses below one edge after decimation or when wedge routing would move it more than attractCm (step 4); or with E_SELF_INTERSECT where their role requires a simple ring: a proposal that cannot be inserted is not a street. The F-NUM ladder of §5.2 applies to optional chains; mandatory chains never reach it.',['§3.6 Chain classes','§3.1 KernelError comments'],'kernel',['@insertChain cls'],['@E_BELOW_MIN_ANGLE','@E_BELOW_MIN_EDGE','@E_SELF_INTERSECT','§5.2 F-NUM ladder','§4.3 S5c growth proposals'],['optional chain','F-NUM'])
it('KERNEL-0256','rule','3.6','Clearance: attraction and the placement rule maintain §3.10 clearance invariant for insertions, and warp checks it for moves. Consequence stages rely on: a setback smaller than minEdge collapses to zero (a building front drawn 10 cm behind the kerb lands on the kerb), so a stage wanting a visible setback draws at least minEdge (§4.7).',['§3.6 Clearance'],'kernel',['@invariants clearance'],['§4.7 setbacks','V02 buildings'],['clearance','setback collapse'])
it('KERNEL-0257','rule','3.6','Roles on shared stretches (GEO-4): when a chain shares an edge (by stretch attraction or an exact collinear overlap) the edge role set gains the chain (role, entity, level) row: never a duplicate edge, never an overwrite. A wall pulled onto a river bank is one edge carrying bank and wall; a quay on a shore carries shore and way.',['§3.6 Roles on shared stretches'],'kernel',['@insertChain step 3','§2.2 role rows'],['§8 renderers reading role sets','§4.8 walls','§4.11 quays'],['role set merge','GEO-4'])
it('KERNEL-0258','mechanism','3.6','Slivers (GEO-4): a face below its class floor (width or area, never minEdge) is resolved by absorbSliver: it merges into the neighbour across its longest shared boundary whose edges carry only partition roles; if every candidate boundary carries a structural role, no edge is deleted and the sliver stays as a face of kind residue owned by the neighbour across its longest boundary (W_SLIVER_RETYPED). A street, wall, shore or border can never lose a stretch to sliver repair; mergeFaces across a structural edge returns E_STRUCTURAL.',['§3.6 Roles on shared stretches, and slivers','§3.4 absorbSliver','§3.5 hourglass row'],'kernel',['@absorbSliver','class floors'],['W_SLIVER_RETYPED','residue face kind (§2.2)','§4.7 plot repair'],['sliver','residue'])
it('KERNEL-0259','mechanism','3.6','splitFace contact semantics (GEO-6): a cut is a polyline whose interior lies in the face open interior and which touches the face boundary exactly at its two endpoints, decided by exact orient2d on lattice points, never a parameter-space epsilon.',['§3.6 splitFace contact semantics'],'kernel',['@orient2d'],['@splitFace','§4.6 cuts'],['cut','GEO-6'])
it('KERNEL-0260','step','3.6','splitFace rule 1: each endpoint is a boundary vertex or a point on a boundary edge inserted by splitThrough; with attraction, an endpoint within attractCm of a boundary vertex lands on it, and so does an endpoint whose foot on a boundary edge lies within attractCm of that edge endpoint (step 2).',['§3.6 splitFace rule 1'],'kernel',['@splitThrough','@attraction'],['@splitFace'],['cut endpoint'])
it('KERNEL-0261','step','3.6','splitFace rule 2: any other contact is E_NOT_TWO_CROSSINGS: the cut passing through or within attractCm of a boundary vertex, grazing a reflex vertex, running within attractCm of a boundary edge anywhere but where it arrives at an endpoint, overlapping a boundary edge, or crossing the boundary more than twice. splitFace never lets attraction bend a cut into a pinch; the stage ladder picks another cut (§4.6).',['§3.6 splitFace rule 2'],'kernel',['@splitFace'],['@E_NOT_TWO_CROSSINGS','§4.6 cut ladder'],['pinch'])
it('KERNEL-0262','step','3.6','splitFace rule 3: an antenna (a dangling chain inside the face - a cul-de-sac spine, a dead-end lane) counts as boundary: a cut may end on it (a declared T-junction, the natural end against a dead-end street) and may not cross it.',['§3.6 splitFace rule 3'],'kernel',[],['@splitFace','§4.6'],['antenna'])
it('KERNEL-0263','step','3.6','splitFace rule 4: a cut from the outer ring to a hole does not split the face; it is connectHole(face, cut), whose post-condition is the same face count and one hole fewer (the slit of §4.6); a violation is E_HOLE_CONTACT.',['§3.6 splitFace rule 4','§3.4 connectHole'],'kernel',['@connectHole'],['@E_HOLE_CONTACT','§4.6 slit'],['slit'])
it('KERNEL-0264','step','3.6','splitFace rule 5: the two children are returned in canonical order, the face left of the directed cut first, so an anchor lying exactly on the cut belongs to the left child (the tie-break of the anchor rule, §6.3).',['§3.6 splitFace rule 5','§3.4 splitFace comment'],'kernel',['@splitFace'],['§6.3 anchor rule'],['left child first'])
it('KERNEL-0265','step','3.6','splitFace rule 6: a face exported area boundary omits its antennae, so a GeoJSON ring never retraces a slit (§11).',['§3.6 splitFace rule 6'],'§11 export (kernel supplies antenna-free ring)',['@splitFace'],['§11 GeoJSON export'],['antenna-free export ring'],restates='§11 export rule')
it('KERNEL-0266','mechanism','3.6','Moves - warp and smoothing (GEO-10, GEO-35, GEO-5 (c)): warp(field, fromLevel, region) applies a displacement field to every vertex of level >= fromLevel inside the region. Shared boundaries are single vertices, so streets, blocks, plots and footprints under a brush move together; vertices of level < fromLevel stay fixed (a street warp does not move the coast unless fromLevel = 0). Smoothing committed geometry is a warp whose field the smoother computes (§3.4), under the same rules.',['§3.6 Moves'],'kernel',['@warp','§7.1 fields'],['§7.1 brushes','smoothing'],['moveValidation','fromLevel','GEO-10'])
it('KERNEL-0267','rule','3.6','A move batch keeps nothing (J-K27): E_KEPT governs insertion and clearing only (§3.3); a move batch rederivesFrom is the first stage of §4.1 order (J-K33); what a move may not displace is decided by the held set, what it may not break by the whole-step validation.',['§3.6 Moves','§3.3 KernelTx.rederivesFrom comment','§3.3 kept content'],'kernel',['@KernelTx.rederivesFrom'],['@heldSet','@wholeStep'],['move keeps nothing','J-K27','J-K33'])
it('KERNEL-0268','mechanism','3.6','Held vertices (GEO-5 (c)): before the step the kernel computes the HELD SET: (1) every vertex of the geometry a locked or pinned entity references resolve to (§7.2) - the level-n route vertices of every ring of its FaceRef faces (outer rings and holes), of its ChainRef and EdgeSetRef edges, its VertexRef vertices - and of every finer face nested in its faces (§7.2 makes every finer face under a locked or pinned entity a fixed input); (2) closed downwards by level: the two endpoints of every coarser edge carrying a held Steiner vertex are held, so that edge does not move and its held vertex keeps its place; each round adds endpoints of a strictly coarser level, so closure ends within four rounds. The field is zero at every held vertex and the dry run lists the held entities, so a locked plot, block or building keeps every corner although none carries a vertex role.',['§3.6 Held vertices'],'kernel',['§7.2 locks and pins','@resolve'],['@wholeStep','§7.1 dry run held list'],['heldSet','held set','four rounds'])
it('KERNEL-0269','rule','3.6','Kernel-pinned vertices under warp: gates, junctions, portals and the vertices smoothing, simplification and cutting keep move with everything else unless held, because a warp is the user explicit move and every entity names its vertices by role (§2.2 rule 5), so a moved gate is still its gate.',['§3.6 Held vertices','§3.4 pinVertices'],'kernel',['@pinVertices'],['@warp'],['pinned vs held'])
it('KERNEL-0270','rule','3.6','Deck and water-gate ends held: a bridge deck_end vertices and a water gate gate_end vertices are held unless fromLevel = 0, because the step that made the crossing fixed it across the water (§2.7): a deck perpendicular to the flow, a water gate wall edge bank to bank - whatever level its bank vertices have (L0 for a deck and an edited water gate; L1 Steiner vertices on the shores for one the trace made at generation, which would otherwise slide along the shore under a street warp and skew the gate edge, §2.2 rule 7; J-K35); with fromLevel = 0 the banks and the crossing move together.',['§3.6 Held vertices'],'kernel',['§2.7 Bridge, Gate','§2.2 rule 7'],['@heldSet'],['deck_end held','gate_end held','J-K35'])
it('KERNEL-0271','mechanism','3.6','Vertices on fixed routes slide (J-K18): a route is FIXED when its edge is of level < fromLevel or both its endpoints are held. A vertex of level >= fromLevel on a fixed route and not held (a street ending on a shore, a bridgehead, a riverside junction - level-1 Steiner vertices on L0 edges under §7.1 default fromLevel - a neighbour footprint corner on a locked plot line) moves only along that route: its displacement is projected onto the route direction at the vertex, the projected point kept at least attractCm from the route endpoints and from every held vertex on it, and the vertex is seated at the lattice point the placement rule chooses for that point ((i) within 0.71 cm of the route, (ii) >= minEdge from the route edge endpoints and its neighbours on the route, (iii) clearance), never the plain nearest lattice point, so the slid vertex keeps §3.10 placement bound and a slide beside a vertex 25.0-25.7 cm from the route commits. A brush over a quay road drags its junctions along the quay instead of halving to a no-op against the shore (fixture §13.1).',['§3.6 Vertices on fixed routes slide'],'kernel',['@placement','@heldSet'],['@wholeStep','§13.1 fixture'],['slide','fixed route','J-K18'])
it('KERNEL-0272','mechanism','3.6','Re-seats (J-K17): each Steiner vertex on a moved coarser edge is re-seated at the lattice point the placement rule chooses for its projection onto the moved route, (i)-(iii) read on the moved geometry (two roundings stack here as on insertion); where no lattice point within attractCm of the projection qualifies, the step fails validation and halves. Measured (fixall_kernel_r3_checks.out A): over 200 000 re-seats beside a vertex 25.0-25.7 cm from the route, plain nearest rounding breaks minEdge or clearance in 12.9 % of cases and the placement rule in none, moving the vertex at most 4.3 cm along the route and keeping it within 0.71 cm; constructive case: a kerb warped to run (0,0)-(1 000,9) past a vertex at (517,30), 25.35 cm from it, with a plot-line end re-seated at that vertex foot - plain rounding lands 24.999 cm away and fails, the placement rule lands 25.999 cm away and the step commits.',['§3.6 Re-seats'],'kernel',['@placement'],['@wholeStep','@CommitRecord moved'],['re-seat','J-K17'])
it('KERNEL-0273','rule','3.6','Order within a step (J-K30): re-seats and slides read vertices that may be moving in the same step, so they are placed one at a time in canonical order by each vertex lattice position before the step (y, then x), each reading the vertices already placed at their placed points and every other moved vertex at its rounded target (a re-seat or slide rounded target = nearest lattice point to its projected point); the whole-step validation then checks the result. The order is a function of positions alone, never slots (§3.3). Measured (fixall_kernel_r4_checks.out B): for two Steiner vertices re-seated onto one warped route with projections 25.0-26.5 cm apart, the two possible orders place different points in 6.8 % of pairs.',['§3.6 Order within a step'],'kernel',['@slotIndependence'],['@wholeStep'],['step order','J-K30'])
it('KERNEL-0274','rule','3.6','The split flag under moves (J-K26): a step clears the split flag (§2.2 rule 3) (b) of every vertex it moves, EXCEPT a Steiner vertex re-seated onto its coarser edge route and a vertex sliding along a fixed route (both still lie on the route of the edge they split; dissolving either restores that edge exactly; a re-seated Steiner vertex keeps its flag so a later clearFiner dissolves it and the next derivation meets the clean coarser edge); and (b-prime) of every flagged vertex it does NOT move that is of the same level as a run it split (a same-level split foot or crossing, or a vertex promoted onto that run) when the nearest vertex of that level or coarser beside it along that run moves in the step (re-seat or slide included): the run line moved from under it. A Steiner vertex of a coarser run keeps its flag (re-seated with that run). So a warped same-level T-junction or crossing becomes a shape vertex of the warped chains, and removing the lane that ended there never straightens the warped spine. The commit record lists the (b-prime) vertices in unflagged; the edit undo record restores their flags (§3.3, §7.4).',['§3.6 The split flag under moves','§3.3 CommitRecord.unflagged'],'kernel',['§2.2 rule 3 split flag'],['@CommitRecord unflagged','§7.4 undo'],['split flag','J-K26',"b'"])
it('KERNEL-0275','mechanism','3.6','Whole-step validation (GEO-10, GEO-35): each target is rounded to the lattice (re-seats and slides by the placement rule, in the order above) and the whole step is validated: every edge incident to a moved vertex (including edges between two moved vertices and edges at re-seated or sliding Steiner vertices) >= minEdge and tested against the index for crossings; every unmoved vertex tested against the triangles swept by the moved edges; no two vertices on one lattice point; no held vertex moved; the clearance invariant holds; and the edit layer recordCheck passes - every held point record inside its host face at its clearance (§7.4), every building whose footprint the step moves valid under §2.6 wing-carrying rule, decided by §2.6 rule 4 tiered test after the kernel own checks pass, stopping at the first failing building in address order.',['§3.6 Whole-step validation'],'kernel',['@queries','@heldSet','@recordCheck','§7.4 held point records','§2.6 rule 4'],['@warp result'],['wholeStep','whole-step validation','swept triangles'])
it('KERNEL-0276','fallback','3.6','Whole-step failure: the step strength is halved globally - deterministic, at most 8 halvings, after which the step is a reported no-op. The commit record lists every moved vertex (§3.3) and §3.10 minEdge row checks the new and moved edges it names, so a pinch or swirl that would shorten an edge to 10 cm, or stack two vertices on one lattice point, halves instead of committing.',['§3.6 Whole-step validation'],'kernel',['@wholeStep'],['§7.1 brushes (pinch, swirl)','@invariants minEdge row','§5.4 report no-op'],['halving','8 halvings'])
it('KERNEL-0277','rule','3.6','Faces a committed move step leaves below a class floor are re-derived locally, each by its own batch whose rederivesFrom is the stage that makes that face, and logged.',['§3.6 after whole-step validation'],'kernel (re-derivation machinery §7.3)',['class floors'],['§7.3 local re-derivation','§5.4 log'],['post-move floor re-derivation'])
it('KERNEL-0278','rule','3.6','The named brushes of §7.1 are displacement fields supplied by the edit layer (a moveVertex: §3.4); a whole-map rotation is a view transform, never a vertex operation, because it would re-snap every vertex in the arena.',['§3.6 after whole-step validation'],'§7.1 editing (kernel constraint)',['§7.1 brushes'],['§8 view transform'],['rotation is a view transform'])
add(I)
